    "body": "UPS agreed to buy Smartways Logistics, a medical courier that handles urgent shipments across Australia and New Zealand. The deal would give UPS Healthcare more options for delivering medical devices, clinical trial materials and radiopharmaceuticals, which are radioactive drugs used in diagnosis and treatment.\nWhat’s Related\nUPS expects the deal to close in the first half of 2027, subject to regulatory reviews and approvals. The company did not disclose the purchase price or other terms.\nSmartways has more than 350 medical couriers and serves more than 1,300 hospitals daily, according to its owner, private equity firm The Riverside Company. UPS says it plans to connect those local courier services with its global healthcare shipping and distribution business .\nHow Smartways handles urgent shipments\nSmartways runs its urgent delivery service around the clock. For an emergency shipment headed from a city to a regional hospital, the company can assign a driver to take it directly to its destination. Its air freight service puts shipments on the next available flight and uses agreements with domestic airlines to load them last and unload them first.\nDispatchers monitor urgent shipments and can change routes if traffic or flight schedules shift. Customers can track the courier by GPS on most urgent deliveries, and Smartways says it alerts customers to disruptions before the scheduled arrival time.\nGetting surgical kits back after use\nSmartways also handles the return trip for loaned surgical instruments. After delivering a kit, the courier company tracks the scheduled surgery date and coordinates with the hospital’s sterile services department to collect it. This helps medical device suppliers get kits back sooner so they can send them out again.\nRiverside invested in Smartways in 2021. Since then, the courier has expanded its delivery coverage across both countries and added services for life sciences companies. It also built out a system that helps hospitals, device makers, and logistics providers coordinate shipments of medical equipment.\nWhere Smartways fits into UPS Healthcare\nUPS already offers temperature-controlled shipping and storage, along with order fulfillment for drug and medical device makers. The Smartways deal would add a local courier business set up to handle deliveries with tight deadlines. UPS did not give a timetable for combining the two companies’ services after the deal closes.\nThe agreement follows the October opening of UPS’s more than $80 million hub at Clark International Airport in the Philippines. The hub has refrigerated and freezer storage, as well as a station where workers can replenish ice in temperature-sensitive shipments while they’re in transit. UPS said its healthcare business reached $3 billion in revenue for the third consecutive quarter in the third quarter of 2026.\nVisit Logistics Management for more coverage of this story.",
